--TEST--
`Core\Http\Response`'s readers are reached as members: a `Core` instance has no property a program can name, whichever member roster the class carries — `rule:http-server/retry-is-opt-in-jittered-and-closed`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*: the accepted spelling and the
// refused one are written against the same value in the same file, so a class
// that grew a reachable field would take the second half of this green while
// the first still passed.
//
// `nvs_stdlib::registry::CoreClass::slots` is the home of the rule — a slot is
// this crate's layout and not a surface — and `crate::http`'s module doc is
// where the decision to leave `Core\Http\Response` inside it was written down.
Core\Http\Target $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://93.184.216.34/ok");

// The accepted side. Refused at run time by the missing transport, which is
// not what this case is about and never gets that far anyway.
try {
    Core\Http\Response $ok = Core\Http\Client::get($pinned);
    echo $ok->status() as string, " ", $ok->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $unsent) {
    echo $unsent->message, "\n";
}

// The refused side, once per reader: the member exists and the property does
// not, so this is `E0405` rather than a name-resolution error.
Core\Http\Response $r = Core\Http\Client::get($pinned);
echo $r->status, "\n";
echo $r->text, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Http\Response` has no property named `status`
   --> case.nvs:24:6
%A
error[E0405]: `Core\Http\Response` has no property named `text`
   --> case.nvs:25:6
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
